From 837b860082fa1953afedc8c5333c7dd793c4ea9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "A.Cervantez" <155030226+Ywach404@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:46:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: the first person gun follows the aiming hand

With hand aim on, a shot went where the controller pointed while the gun stayed where head look put it, so nothing on screen showed where a shot would go. A second m9 row (tools/m9/handaim.toml, cell 58) turns the view render's call to the view model's pose at client+0x19146e (client+0x190ba0, ret 0x1c, no relocation in the call) into a call through the cell. HandAimViewModel calls the original with its seven arguments and, on the stock path only (the record's unlagged angles at +0x20 equal the view's angles passed in, which the path that copies another entity's pose does not give), turns the record's angles at +0x14 and +0x20 by the same offset the command gets, computed from zero so the game's pitch limit never eats part of it, and moves its origin at +8 by the hand's place from the head.

The runtime had no head position, so the host now gives it beside the angles (rt_host_head_position, under a sequence word like the hand slots) and the head pump takes it with the angles, so the shim reads the position the view was drawn for. The offset is taken in the head's level frame, so the head's heading cancels, and turned by the view's yaw into the game's world (rt_headlook_hand_offset, tested in the input selftest). It is scaled by RT_STEREO_UNITS_PER_M as the backend reads it, less RT_HANDAIM_REST (forward,right,up in game units, 0 by default: the model's own origin then sits at the hand). Position needs the hand tracked with position (state 2 or 3); RT_HANDAIM_POS=0 keeps the turn and drops the move. rt_input_hand_aim_pose now returns the hand's tracking state so the caller can tell.

Hand aim stays behind RT_HANDAIM=1 until a headset run shows the gun where the shots go. test_uppatch's hand aim parity checks both rows by id against handaim.c, including the call's target and the binding's opcode.
